@* macOS: the renderer.
The renderer is where the two graphics interfaces meet the program, and so it is where
the port differs most from the original. It still does the same three things: it owns the
GPU objects, it draws the scene into an off-screen multisampled image, and it hands the
result to the user interface. Before reading the code it helps to have a dictionary.

\medskip
\begin{tabular}{ll}
\textbf{Direct3D 11} & \textbf{Metal} \\
device (|ID3D11Device|) & device (|MTL::Device|) \\
immediate context & command queue, command buffers and \emph{encoders} \\
swap chain & the drawables of a |CAMetalLayer| (owned here by |MTKView|) \\
texture + shader resource view & texture, possibly a view of another texture \\
render target and depth-stencil views & attachments of a \emph{render pass descriptor} \\
|ClearRenderTargetView| & load action |Clear| \\
|ResolveSubresource| & store action |MultisampleResolve| \\
shaders + input layout + blend state & one \emph{render pipeline state} \\
rasterizer state & a few calls on the encoder (|setCullMode|, \dots) \\
depth-stencil state, sampler state & the same, created from descriptors \\
dynamic constant buffer (|Map|/|Unmap|) & |setVertexBytes| / |setFragmentBytes| \\
|ComPtr| & |NS::SharedPtr| \\
\end{tabular}
\medskip

The deepest difference is in the second row. A Direct3D~11 context executes each call as
it is made, as far as the program can tell. Metal makes the recording of work explicit:
the program asks the queue for a \emph{command buffer}, records passes into it through
encoders, and \emph{commits} it; the GPU executes committed buffers in order. In this
program every frame has exactly one command buffer, which receives first the scene and
then the user interface.

